description:
The sidebar TOC ("On this page" + entries) always renders in the body typeface, even when a theme or _brand.yml sets a distinct headings face. There is no supported knob to change it.

## Cause (ported Q1 behaviour, not a regression)

resources/scss/bootstrap/_bootstrap-rules.scss (the block containing `font-family: inherit;`, around lines 1908,3007) has:

    .sidebar .toc-actions h2, .sidebar .quarto-code-links h2, .sidebar .quarto-other-links h2,
    .sidebar .quarto-alternate-notebooks h2, .sidebar .quarto-alternate-formats h2,
    .sidebar nav[role="doc-toc"] > h2 {
      font-weight: 500; ... font-family: inherit; ...
    }

The TOC title is an h2, so Bootstrap's reboot gives it $headings-font-family; this rule then forces `inherit`, so it takes the body face from <body>. The TOC entries are plain `ul li a` and inherit the body face too. Verified on a live page (EB Garamond body / Merriweather Sans headings via _brand.yml): computed font-family on nav#TOC, its h2, and its links is EB Garamond; main h2 is Merriweather Sans. Same rule exists verbatim in Quarto 1 (external-sources/quarto-cli/src/resources/formats/html/bootstrap/_bootstrap-rules.scss ~L1213-1225), so this is parity, and the default should not change.

Quarto exposes $toc-font-size, $toc-color, $toc-active-border, $toc-inactive-border, $toc-tools-font-size (resources/scss/bootstrap/_bootstrap-variables.scss:51-56) but no font-family variable for the sidebar.

## Proposed change

1. Add `$toc-font-family: inherit !default;` (and possibly `$toc-font-weight: 500 !default;`) next to the other $toc-* variables.
2. Use it in the rule above instead of the literal `inherit` (for the doc-toc h2), and set `font-family: $toc-font-family` on `.sidebar nav[role="doc-toc"]` so entries follow it as well. Consider the same variable for the sibling other-links / code-links / alternate-formats h2s, or a separate $toc-tools-font-family.
3. Default stays `inherit`, so existing output is byte-identical unless a theme sets the variable.
4. Optional follow-up: decide whether brand typography.headings should feed it (probably not by default — Q1 intent is that the sidebar reads as UI chrome, not document headings — but a theme author can then write `$toc-font-family: $headings-font-family`).

## User-side workaround (works today)

    /*-- scss:rules --*/
    .sidebar nav[role="doc-toc"] {
      font-family: $headings-font-family;
      font-weight: $headings-font-weight;
    }

Found 2026-09-08 while styling cscheid-net-2026 with _brand.yml.

**Discovered during**: q2-preview Plan 2A review (2026-05-07).

## The bug

`crates/wasm-quarto-hub-client/src/lib.rs:1208-1214` (single-doc render path) and `:1364-1369` (project render path) loop over `ctx.artifacts` and write each artifact's content to VFS:

```rust
for (_key, artifact) in ctx.artifacts.iter() {
    if let Some(artifact_path) = &artifact.path {
        let vfs_path = resolver.on_disk_path_for(artifact.scope, artifact_path);
        runtime.add_file(&vfs_path, artifact.content.clone());
    }
}
```

There is no guard against empty content. `ResourceCollectorTransform` (in `crates/quarto-core/src/transforms/resource_collector.rs:65-71`) produces artifact entries via `Artifact::from_path(...)`, which sets `content: Vec::new()` (`crates/quarto-core/src/artifact.rs:108-116`). The artifact's `path` field is `base_dir.join(url)` — typically absolute in WASM context.

`Path::join` semantics: an absolute second argument replaces the first. So `vfs_root.join("/project/hero.png")` collapses to `/project/hero.png`, which is also where `automergeSync` (in `hub-client/src/services/automergeSync.ts:84,87`) wrote the user's image bytes via `vfsAddFile` / `vfsAddBinaryFile`.

**Result**: the flush overwrites the user's upload with empty bytes on every render that references the image.

## How it surfaces

Today, in HTML preview, embedded images may appear broken on second-and-subsequent renders. Hasn't been widely reported — possibly because users don't often embed local images, or because path semantics in some cases avoid the collision (the bug is conditional on whether `ctx.document.input` is absolute or relative; both occur depending on how JS calls the WASM entry).

For q2-preview (Plan 2A), the iframe rewriter explicitly relies on the user's upload bytes being present. Without the fix, q2-preview renders with embedded images would produce empty data: URIs after the first render.

## Fix

Add an `is_empty()` guard to both flush loops:

```rust
for (_key, artifact) in ctx.artifacts.iter() {
    if let Some(artifact_path) = &artifact.path {
        if !artifact.content.is_empty() {
            let vfs_path = resolver.on_disk_path_for(artifact.scope, artifact_path);
            runtime.add_file(&vfs_path, artifact.content.clone());
        }
    }
}
```

This preserves manifest semantics for `ResourceCollectorTransform` (the entries still exist in `ctx.artifacts` for downstream consumers like `ResourceReportStage`) while preventing the overwrite. Theme CSS / icon CSS / fonts (which use `Artifact::from_bytes` with real content) keep flushing correctly.

## Tests

- Strengthen the website-fixture test in `crates/quarto-core/tests/render_page_in_project.rs::website_q2_preview_renders_through_orchestrator`: pre-populate VFS at the user's expected upload path with non-empty bytes, run the render, assert bytes are unchanged. (Today's test asserts only that the manifest entry exists.)
- Add a similar regression test for `RenderToHtmlRenderer` if not already present.

## Plan references

- Plan 2A §"Risk areas → Empty-content artifact overwrite (latent bug discovered during plan review)" — `claude-notes/plans/2026-05-04-q2-preview-plan-2a-iframe-foundation.md`
- Plan 1 §"Multi-plan contract: page-scoped image artifacts" — `claude-notes/plans/2026-05-04-q2-preview-plan-1-pipeline.md`
- Plan 1 §Test plan, "Website fixture with embedded image" — references this issue for the deferred 6th assertion (bytes survive render).

