RETRACTION — the generalisation I proposed earlier is wrong as worded. Flagged by the q2-side session implementing the fix, and I have verified it.

I wrote: "A cheap guard would be a test asserting that for each item source, a listing built purely from that source and carrying no explicit sort: renders in declared order."

That assertion is FALSE for the document/glob source, and deliberately so. A document-only listing with no explicit sort: is supposed to come out `order asc, title asc`, in Q1 and q2 alike. Measured on q2 0.27.0 with a glob-only listing over posts/{a,b,c}.qmd titled Zulu/Alpha/Mike — so that filename order and title order disagree:

    file order  Zulu | Alpha | Mike
    rendered    Alpha | Mike | Zulu

Landing my test as worded would either fail immediately on the glob source or, worse, be "fixed" by suppressing the default sort for document listings — which would break parity for every glob listing on every site. I generalised from the record case without checking the case I was generalising over.

THE INVARIANT THAT DOES GENERALISE is one level up, and it is the q2 session's formulation, not mine: not "declared order for every source", but "every source carries an explicit, reviewed decision about whether it arms the default sort". Encoded as a forcing function rather than a test —

    enum ListingItemSource { Document, MetadataDocument, Metadata }
    impl ListingItemSource {
        fn arms_default_sort(self) -> bool {
            match self {
                Self::Document | Self::MetadataDocument => true,
                Self::Metadata => false,
            }
        }
    }

— exhaustive match, no wildcard arm, so a fourth variant does not compile until someone answers the question for it, with tests pinning each variant's answer.

That is strictly better than what I proposed, for the reason they give: a test only fails once someone has already written the new source, and can be made green by deleting it. A non-exhaustive match would let the next item source repeat this bug silently, which is precisely the failure mode the "where the gap came from" comment above describes.

Correcting it here because the wrong version is recorded in both skeins as a recommendation, and it is the kind of thing that gets picked up and implemented later without re-derivation.

description:
A page that renders no table of contents still reserves the right margin column for one, so its content is narrower and mis-centred compared with Quarto 1. Roughly 150-200px of permanently empty grid track is held open. Two independent emission gaps produce this, and they need separate fixes: Q1 applies one remedy or the other depending on the page, never both.

Neither is a stylesheet problem. The relevant rules are byte-identical between Q1's compiled `bootstrap-*.min.css` and q2's `quarto-theme-*.css`:

    body.docked.fullcontent .page-columns{ ... grid-template-columns:
      ... [body-content-start] minmax(500px, calc(1000px - 3em))
        [body-content-end] 1.5em [body-end body-end-outset page-end-inset page-end]
        5fr [screen-end-inset] 1.5em [screen-end]}

    .page-columns .column-page-right{grid-column:body-content-start/page-end; ...}

Without `fullcontent`, `body.docked .page-columns` instead keeps `50px [body-end-outset] minmax(50px, 100px) [page-end-inset] 50px` between `body-end` and `page-end`. The CSS is fully ported; the classes that select it are never emitted.

GAP 1 — `fullcontent` is unreachable for any page that is in a sidebar.

`crates/quarto-core/src/template.rs:964-977`:

    let mut classes: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    match (from_meta, has_toc) {
        (Some(s), _) if !s.is_empty() => classes.push(s),
        (Some(_), _) | (None, true) => {}
        (None, false) => classes.push("fullcontent".to_string()),
    }

`from_meta` is `rendered.navigation.body-classes`, written by `SidebarRenderTransform` (e.g. `"nav-sidebar docked"`). When it is present the first arm matches and `has_toc` is never consulted, so `fullcontent` can never be appended to a sidebar page. Quarto 1 treats the two as orthogonal and composes both, producing `<body class="nav-sidebar docked nav-fixed fullcontent quarto-light">`. Its decision (`src/format/html/format-html-bootstrap.ts:1044-1071`) keys only on whether the right margin has content:

    if (!hasRightContent && !hasMarginContent && !hasToc) {
      doc.body.classList.add("fullcontent");
    }

The regression has a traceable origin: `bd-mgoh` replaced a hardcoded `body class="fullcontent"` with the sidebar-supplied class, and expressing the result as a match on a two-tuple turned "compose" into "choose". `7263712d4 fix(template): suppress fullcontent class when TOC is rendered` added the `has_toc` arm without restoring composition.

Fix direction: make the two independent — take the structural base from `from_meta` when present, and *additionally* push `fullcontent` whenever `!has_toc` and no margin content is rendered.

GAP 2 — `page-layout: full` never sets a column class on `<main>`.

q2's `<main>` class is a literal in the HTML template, `crates/quarto-core/src/template.rs:324`:

    <main class="content$if(rendered.title-block-banner)$ quarto-banner-title-block$endif$" id="quarto-document-content">

`page-layout` reaches only the container class at `template.rs:292` (`page-layout-$page-layout$`). Grepping `crates/` for `column-page-right` returns nothing — q2 has no equivalent pass at all. Quarto 1, `src/format/html/format-html-bootstrap.ts:1008-1019`:

    const fullLayout = formatHasFullLayout(format);
    if (fullLayout) {
      // ...drop an empty left sidebar...
      const column = suggestColumn(doc);
      setMainColumn(doc, column);
    }

and `suggestColumn` (`:1959-1983`) chooses by which margins are actually occupied — left and right used gives `column-body`; left only gives `column-page-right`; right only gives `column-page-left`; neither gives `column-page`.

Fix direction: port `suggestColumn`/`setMainColumn` — under `page-layout: full`, compute `<main>`'s column class from whether the left sidebar and the right margin actually have content.

Worth being precise about what each class buys, because only one of them widens anything. `fullcontent` does not raise the content ceiling: `body.docked` and `body.docked.fullcontent` both cap the content column at `minmax(500px, calc(1000px - 3em))`. What it does is release the reserved right tracks to the flexible `5fr` gutters, which re-centres the page and, at intermediate viewport widths where the gutters are already at their minimum, is what lets the content reach its cap instead of being squeezed short of it. `column-page-right` is the one that genuinely widens the content, spanning `<main>` from `body-content-start` to `page-end`, past the cap.

Real-world hit — the Positron website (positron.posit.co docs), 98-page site render, q2 0.28.0 vs the Quarto 1 baseline. Pages carrying `fullcontent`: Q1 `ai-features`, `index`, `features`; q2 `index`, `features`, `404`. Pages carrying `main.column-page-right`: Q1 `welcome` (1); q2 none (0).

- `welcome.qmd` (`page-layout: full`, `toc: false`) is GAP 2 and is the reported symptom: the main column is visibly too narrow because space is held for a TOC that does not exist.
- `ai-features.qmd` (`toc: false`, in the sidebar) is GAP 1.
- `index.qmd` and `features.qmd` are `toc: false` but not in any sidebar, and q2 already handles them correctly. That is the useful control: q2's `fullcontent` logic works; only its composition with a sidebar fails.
- `404.html` differs in the opposite direction — it has no headings, so q2 collapses the column while Q1 reserves it. That is arguably q2 being more correct and is deliberately out of scope here.

Repro: /Users/gordon/src/q2-positron-docs/llms-info/repros/no-toc-reserves-margin-column/

    cd /Users/gordon/src/q2-positron-docs/llms-info/repros/no-toc-reserves-margin-column
    sh check.sh            # optional arg: path to the q2 binary

It renders a small website with a docked sidebar under q2 and then under Quarto 1, and prints the `<body>` class, the `<main>` class, and whether a margin sidebar was emitted, for four pages: A1 in the sidebar with a TOC (both agree, control), A2 in the sidebar with `toc: false` (Q1 adds `fullcontent`, q2 does not — GAP 1), A3 in the sidebar with `toc: false` and `page-layout: full` (Q1 sets `main class="content column-page-right"`, q2 does not — GAP 2), A4 not in the sidebar with `toc: false` (both add `fullcontent`, control).

Origin: `br-no-toc-reserves-margin-column-71ga13xk` in the Positron port's skein. Observed on q2 0.28.0 and still present at q2 HEAD (103af4445, 115 commits past the v0.28.0 tag); `crates/quarto-core/src/template.rs` is untouched since the v0.28.0 tag.

Note for whoever picks this up: the text-diff, asset-check and link-check sweeps the porting projects run cannot see this. It is a class-attribute diff only — the markup is well-formed, no asset is missing, and the extracted text is identical between engines.

repo-actions shipped in 0.27.0 (bd-repo-actions-missing-99ezd2fe) and the markup half is complete and correct. The page-footer copy, however, has no styles: q2 ships nothing scoped to `.nav-footer .toc-actions`, so those links render as a browser-default bulleted list instead of Q1's centred horizontal row.

Markup vs CSS, measured on a two-page website with repo-actions: [edit, source, issue]:

                 toc-action links   toc-actions containers   .nav-footer .toc-actions rules
  q2 0.27.0             6                    2                          0
  Q1 99.9.9             6                    2                          8

The markup columns are identical — the emitted HTML is byte-for-byte Q1's. The entire defect is the last column.

## The missing source

`~/src/quarto-cli/src/resources/projects/website/navigation/quarto-nav.scss:770-793`, in that file's `/*-- scss:rules --*/` layer:

    .nav-footer .toc-actions {
      a,
      a:hover { text-decoration: none; }

      ul {
        display: flex;
        list-style: none;

        :first-child { margin-left: auto; }
        :last-child  { margin-right: auto; }

        li {
          padding-right: 1.5em;
          i.bi { padding-right: 0.4em; }
        }
        li:last-of-type { padding-right: 0; }
      }

      padding-bottom: 0.5em;
      padding-top: 0.5em;
    }

`display: flex` + `list-style: none` produces the visible symptom; the `:first-child`/`:last-child` `margin: auto` pair centres the row.

NO SCSS VARIABLES ARE INVOLVED — every value is a literal, so this block can be ported verbatim with nothing lost. That is worth stating because the SIDEBAR `.toc-actions` rules are different in kind: they live in `_bootstrap-rules.scss` and are variable-driven/themed. The two are deliberately different (flex row vs vertical list) and should not be unified or deduped.

In Q1 this file is always included for every page of a website project — `websiteNavigationSassBundle()` at src/project/types/website/website-navigation.ts:1512-1524 wraps it, and line 272 pushes it unconditionally as the first bundle, ahead of the navbar/sidebar-disable checks. Its dependency is kBootstrapDependencyName, which is why the rules land in the bootstrap-*.min.css bundle rather than a separate stylesheet.

## Scope is small — the surrounding footer layout is already fine

Checked, so the fix does not land in a broken shell: q2 already has the general three-column footer layout that `.toc-actions` sits inside. Rendered-CSS selector counts, q2 vs Q1: `.nav-footer-center` 8 vs 8, `.footer-items` 7 vs 7. The only difference is `.nav-footer-contents` (0 vs 1), and that class appears in zero HTML files in either engine, so it is inert.

The `d-sm-block d-md-none` responsive behaviour needs nothing either — those are stock Bootstrap utility classes, not Quarto CSS.

So this is the 8 rules and nothing else.

## Why it was missed

q2's SCSS does carry `.toc-actions` rules at resources/scss/bootstrap/_bootstrap-rules.scss:1816-1948 — but every scoped one is `.sidebar .toc-actions`, and `grep -rn 'nav-footer' resources/scss/` returns no `toc` hits. The implementation plan (claude-notes/plans/2026-08-24-repo-actions.md:63) cites that exact line range under a heading "What q2 already has", which is true for the TOC copy and not for the footer copy. Nothing in the plan mentions footer styling, so the second emission site got markup and inherited no styles.

## Real-world hit

Every page of the Positron website docs that has a footer: the repo-actions row is bulleted and left-aligned instead of centred and inline. It was invisible to that port's whole sweep toolkit — visible-text diffing cannot distinguish a bulleted list from a flex row, and the asset/link checkers do not read CSS. Found by looking at the rendered page.

## Repro

/Users/gordon/src/q2-positron-docs/llms-info/repros/repo-actions-footer-unstyled/

`q2 render`, then `quarto render --output-dir _site-q1`, then compare markup against CSS:

    grep -o 'class="toc-action"'       _site/index.html | wc -l
    grep -o 'class="toc-actions[^"]*"' _site/index.html | wc -l
    grep -rho '\.nav-footer[^{}]*toc-action[^{}]*{' _site --include='*.css' | wc -l

Origin strand in the Positron-docs porting skein: br-repo-actions-footer-unstyled-ty1os83n. Observed with q2 0.27.0 (official release build).

Found in Tail-Winds corpus validation: shortcode parameter without quotes that starts with a number followed by non-numeric character.

Example file:
- team/index.qmd: {{< fa envelope size=1x >}}

Error: Parse error - unexpected character or token (no code)
Location: At the parameter value '1x' (digit followed by letter, unquoted)

Minimal reproduction: {{< a k=1b >}}

Proposed fix strategy:
- Add error code (e.g., Q-2-34)
- Error message: 'Shortcode parameter values starting with digits must be quoted'
- Hint: 'Use quotes around the parameter value: k="1b"'

Example:
Bad:  {{< fa envelope size=1x >}}
Good: {{< fa envelope size="1x" >}}
